\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{An invertible curved operator needs no kernel projection}
\date{2026-11-17}

\p{For the rational sign model in \ref{fdip-00SY}, take
#{\mathcal N=C^*(X;\mathbb Q)}. Its operator is invertible, so the
[kernel complement construction](fdip-00T1) gives
#{H_U=0} and #{E_U=\extD_U^{-1}}. The quaternionic model
in \ref{fdip-00SZ} has the same property. At trivial links on
that one-vertex quaternionic torus, #{\extD=0}; then
#{\mathcal N=0}, #{E=0} and #{H=\bbI}. The complement construction
thus includes both of these extreme kernels.}
